#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198096 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c23caadd-1738-549f-aade-2c983dc250dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Truth Set of a Predicate"}] :question/id #uuid "4b253527-f5ca-5927-97f0-d91491d598b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12721" :question/problem "Find the truth set of the following predicate:\n\n$\\qquad$ $P(x)$: $x^2+2x=3$\n\nwhere the universal set is $\\mathbb{Z}$." :question/worked-solution "The *truth set* of a predicate $P(x)$ is the set of elements in its universal set $U$ such that substituting these elements for the variable makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{x \\in U \\: \\mid \\: P(x) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is\n\n$$\nU = \\mathbb{Z}\n$$\n\nWe need to find $x \\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\nx^2+2x=3\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the above equation by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2x &= 3 \\\\\nx^{2} + 2x - 3 &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 3)(x - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=-3\\in\\mathbb Z\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx=1\\in\\mathbb Z\n$$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\nT = \\{-3,1\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198097 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa2f2ccb-45b0-5d6a-aa94-ac3eb95c745b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Predicates of More Than One Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "14dc17f1-056e-5e82-81e6-c3964b7df2d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-12722" :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical predicates?\n\n1. $x \\geq y$\n2. $\\dfrac{p}{q}$ is a rational number.\n3. $a$ is a positive number." :question/worked-solution "A *predicate* of the variables $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ is a sentence that contains the variables defined on some *universal set,* and has the property that when we substitute concrete values for all the variables into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options.\n\n- Option I is a predicate of two variables. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(x,y)$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n- Option II is a predicate of two variables. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(p,q)$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n- Option III is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $a$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198098 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f9703bd-92ae-51f7-902b-251a7947ba6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Truth Set of a Multivariable Predicate"}] :question/id #uuid "3d212cb2-394b-5f9d-8e7d-8406ec7ca580" :question/math-academy-id "e-12723" :question/problem "Find the truth set of the following predicate:\n\n$\\qquad$ $p \\, \\mid \\, q$,\n\nwhere the universal set is $\\big\\{(p,q) \\: \\mid \\: p = 1,2,6, \\: q = 3,6,9 \\big\\}$." :question/worked-solution "The *truth set* of a predicate $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is the set of $n$-tuples in its universal set\n\n$$\nU = U_1 \\times U_2 \\times \\cdots \\times U_n\n$$\n\nsuch that substituting these tuples for the variables makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\in U \\: \\mid \\: P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU &= {1, 2, 6} \\cdot {3, 6, 9} \\\\\n&= {(1, 3), (1, 6), (1, 9), (2, 3), (2, 6), (2, 9), (6, 3), (6, 6), (6, 9)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to find $(p,q) \\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ $p \\, \\mid \\, q$\n\nThe following pairs give the elements of our truth set:\n\n- $(1,3)$ since $1$ divides $3$,\n- $(1,6)$ since $1$ divides $6$,\n- $(1,9)$ since $1$ divides $9$,\n- $(2,6)$ since $2$ divides $6$,\n- $(6,6)$ since $6$ divides $6$.\n\nTherefore, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{(1,3), (1,6), (1,9), (2,6), (6,6) \\big\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa2f2ccb-45b0-5d6a-aa94-ac3eb95c745b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Predicates of More Than One Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85e36aba-20ab-580f-8649-7d71a62fa82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194953} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd5dc58-fdce-54fb-a27d-46f0653100fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194954} {:answer/id #uuid "c119dce4-1089-58ac-9f86-ebf1b3adfa48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194955} {:answer/id #uuid "de752fe8-aed1-5018-9c65-e53d94edcc12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194956} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb24f71-a1d2-5e44-a87d-d4a185324549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194957}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ae34e108-41d0-5bc4-99fd-2c4a3cc71e49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194952}] :question/id #uuid "d3a9664a-054f-5dcf-9b0e-e1b4ab3dd171" :question/math-academy-id "q-174456" :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical predicates?\n\n1. $x < y$\n2. $n$ is a perfect square.\n3. Are $n$ and $m$ coprime?"}]}
